    "math_academy_id": "q-87612",
    "knowledge_point_id": "5a72dae6-2bbf-5e59-b71d-8ee57f589753",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Data Set",
    "problem": "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n\n$$\n7,\\,3,\\,3,\\,5,\\,1,\\,0,\\,2\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "The standard deviation of a data set ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\,\\,\\,\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=7$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (7+3+3+5+1+0+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot ({7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (49+9+9+25+1+0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{7}-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{7}-9 \\\\  & =\\frac{97-63}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{34}{7}}.\n$$",
